7. THE DOCK LEAD'S PULSE AND DUTY CAPABILITY (E11-38's wiring half; the 813 contact is the maker's question, clarification/preci-dip-813.txt)
   the lead: 60 mm of 24 AWG from board E's J_BLK to the dock block's land (ASSEMBLY.md section 4), copper d = 0.5106 mm (the AWG definition), A = 0.2047 mm2 = 404.0 circular mils = 317.3 square mils;
   ECSS-Q-ST-30-11C Rev.2 Table C-1: 105 mOhm/m at 20 C, minimum diameter 0.8 mm (the insulated wire).
   Onderdonk (the note's general form, I = A sqrt(log10(1 + (Tm - Ta)/(234 + Ta)) / (33 t)), A in circular mils, t in s, Tm 1083 C); the form reproduces the note's worked example at 177.8 A
   (178 A printed, with the note's own '234 - Ta' spelling; this record uses 234 + Ta, the smaller and conservative figure: 26457 against 30675 A at 70 C).
   the fusing current of the 24 AWG at the short's 4.5 us: 27865 A from 25 C, 26457 A from 70 C, 26018 A from 85 C (the spring's limit); the short's 566 A is 2.14 % of the 70 C figure,
   its 1.441 A2s is 1/2186 of the wire's 3150 A2s to melt at that time; the adiabatic rise of the copper from the pulse: 0.214 K (ECSS's resistance per metre over copper's 385 J/kgK and 8960 kg/m3).
   the eFuse's retry duty (cases (3) and (4) of L4-E11 17a): at most 1.802 A for at most 1.5 s then off at least 0.5 s, a duty of at most 0.75: 1.802 A is 53 % of ECSS Annex C's 3.4 A single-wire
   rating of AWG 24 (a 70 C environment, 150 C wire, radiation alone, in vacuum), the duty's rms 1.561 A is 46 %; Preece's steady fusing current of the bare wire 29.2 A.
   the continuous draw: 1.3208 A declared by L4-E11 18b (39 % of 3.4 A), 1.3208 A recomputed here with the picked mixers at full speed (39 %). The 50 K rule of 6.32.4a cannot be applied: the maker's maximum rating of the fitted wire is not named in ASSEMBLY.md (24 AWG only).
   So the wiring is not the limiting element of the branch on these published relations; the 813 contact's pulse capability (the piston-to-barrel interface, the gold plating, the spring)
   is printed nowhere (3.5 A operating, 10 mOhm static; the SMD series alone prints a 7 A peak) and stays E11-38's bench item and the drafted question.

